# Line endings: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every
# machine.
#
# `text=auto` is the half that normalises on commit, which was already
# happening. `eol=lf` is the half that matters here, because it decides the
# WORKING TREE and overrides a contributor's core.autocrlf -- Git for Windows
# sets that to true when it installs, so a Windows checkout got CRLF
# everywhere and nothing in the repo said otherwise.
#
# What that broke was `gofmt -l .`, which reported every one of the 219 Go
# files as needing formatting, because gofmt normalises CRLF to LF. That made
# the close-out check in CLAUDE.md impossible to pass on Windows, and is why
# CI ran gofmt on the Linux leg only -- a workaround that hid the question of
# what else a CRLF checkout reaches.
#
# The answer, measured on Windows 11 with a genuinely CRLF checkout, is
# NOTHING ELSE:
#
#   - Nothing embedded in Go SOURCE can carry CRLF, by two separate
#     mechanisms, and it is worth keeping them apart. A multi-line raw literal
#     is immune because Go DISCARDS \r from raw string literals -- the
#     language spec says so, and a genuinely CRLF source file compiled here
#     yielded "line one\nline two". Text built with \n escapes in an
#     interpreted literal is immune because the newline is written by the
#     program and never read from the file. The vm engine's boot script and
#     the EKS token-webhook kubeconfig are the first kind; the k3s mirror's
#     hosts.toml and registries.yaml are the second. So all of them reach
#     their Linux containers as LF however the repo was checked out.
#     `TestARealInstanceBootsRunsUserDataAndStaysUp` passes against a real
#     alpine container on a CRLF tree, which is what proved the boot script
#     rather than a reading of the spec.
#   - The only files Nimbus reads from disk at runtime are ones Nimbus wrote
#     itself, so they carry whatever it wrote.
#   - The visualizer's static files are embedded with //go:embed and reach the
#     browser byte for byte, CRLF included, which JavaScript and CSS both
#     accept as a line terminator.
#
# So this file buys one thing: gofmt behaving the same on all three legs. That
# is worth having anyway -- a formatting check that cannot run on a platform
# is a formatting check that will drift there.
* text=auto eol=lf
